 we don't have at  the moment and was the big problem in  the fashion industry is a lack of and no  global legislation to go to go with that  to protect people and we're only just  seeing the rise of global trade unions  to try and ameliorate the rate some of  these processes it's similar to what's  happening happening in fact with  internet law in many cases where because  you have things taking place on the  Internet in kind of the abstract sense  that cross country lines it can be hard  to legislate in a way that both make  sense based on how the internet is  actually used but also takes into  consideration the kind of multinational  impacts of what is happening on the  Internet and similarly when we have a  situation where an American company  presumably American company is sourcing  their clothes from other countries where  the labor practices may well be legal in  that country al biet unethical how do  you create a system of law that takes  that into consideration given that the  eventual vendor of the cup clothing is  in the United States where those  practices would be illegal  yeah I think that companies are  responsible and legally responsible for  their supply chain because they are they  are the ultimate factor in that supply  chain they have the ultimate control  over things like wages and Environmental  Protection and all the standards and so  you have to you know I'd like to see  there being legislation about goods that  come into the UK you know that clothes  that come into the into the UK and a  much more transparent set of supply  chains as well there's a movement here  in the US and I don't know if it also  exists in the UK which is that of  produced locally right and sometimes the  defenders of the globalized apparel  status quo so to speak will say well if  you think that that American Apparel  white t-shirt is expensive now just wait  until it's produced here in the United  States paying us minimum wages how would  you respond to someone who  then the status quo with that type of  argument mm well I mean I'm certainly  not in favor of pitting you know workers  in Bangladesh against workers in you  know in Britain or America I mean we've  seen in Britain and indeed in America  the gradual running down of  manufacturing you know Britain used to  be a huge manufacturing hub as did  America and you know successive  governments have had a deliberate policy  of running that down and that's  certainly true for the for the garment  industry and the key reason you know why  companies don't manufacture in Britain  and in America is is because of wages so  when I would like to see wages rise in  you know across Asia I'm in favor of the  beige you know living wage campaigns and  the Asian the Asia floor wage that try  and create a standard and minimum  standard for the production of all of  our grid there's another kind of layer  to this that for me only exacerbates the  problems with the wages that are being  paid to the workers in many of these  countries and maybe people don't know  this the margins on individual clothing  items are just huge I mean people may  not know that a luxury handbag sells for  10 to 12 times what it costs to produce  we're talking about a thousand or twelve  hundred percent markup I mean many  dresses and shirts are selling for  exponentially more than what the cost is  to produce and this to me only further  infuriates me in terms of not only are  the workers being swindled and maybe  that it's actually worse than being  swindled but the buyers are also paying  a price that makes no sense whatsoever  and somehow the market is bearing that  price hmm yes certainly I mean it's like  you're getting exploitation both at the  point of production and at the point of  consumption and we know with with prices  being quite so high but only on a sort  of positive side as it were  they've been various studies that are  showing that in order to double why  for garment workers all you have to do  is increase the price of sale address by  between 1 and 3% and then amazing  because you would only have to increase  absurdly inflated prices by 1 to 3% to  double the wages that are being paid  it's incredible isn't it it is  incredible and it you know it just shows  just how greedy these corporations are  you know companies like Burberry who  which are billion-dollar billion-dollar  companies or Marks and Spencers that  makes you know over 600 million pounds  in profit every single year and yet they  won't pay a living wage either in  Bangladesh or in you know in the shops  as well in in places like in London here  I would actually be much less offended  by the prices that are charged by the  companies if I knew that they valued the  employees in a way that they really  redistributed the profits to those  employees in a significant way in other  words I just don't want both being  absurdly embarrassingly overcharged for  items I know cost a couple of dollars to  produce and knowing that that the  majority of that money is not going to  the workers yeah no I mean yes I mean I  completely agree it just shows you know  where the priorities of these come of  these companies are which is you know is  not with the people that are on making  them these gigantic profits what  clothing buying practices can the  average person learn that can at least  mitigate how much they're exacerbating  this problem and I word it that way  because if you're buying clothing it's  difficult to not be a part of the  problem at all to completely remove  yourself but what advice can you give to  people mmm  well I mean I like like personally in  the in the work that I do I I encourage  people not just to think of themselves  as consumers and I encourage people to  think of the the action that they do and  the help you know in the sort of  solidarity they show with garment  workers not to be you know when  they're in the shops I don't think you  can change the system we live in by  shopping differently and I don't think  we can buy the kind of justice that we  that we all want to see so I'm much more  concerned with getting people involved  in you know like trade union solidarity  campaigns and in like you know picketing  and pressurizing a big corporations like  like Walmart Walmart and Gap who have  refused to even sign the Bangladesh  Accord on building and fire safety I'm  asking if you've got you know if you've  got a choice between two different  garments and you could afford to make  that choice then of course make the  choice that's least harmful to people  and planet but for many people there you  know there's not a great deal of choice  involved in what they buy and I think  those people have the right to be  involved in in changing the world as  well I'll remind our audience the book  is stitched up the anti-capitalist book  of fashion we've been speaking with the  book's author tansy Hoskins great to  have you on really a great topic and  something I think it's important for  people to know about thank you thank you  thank David
 

